❓Question
Given two strings s and t, return true if t is an anagram of s, otherwise return false.
An anagram contains the same characters with the same frequency, but the order can be different.

Example
Input:  s = "anagram", t = "nagaram"
Output: true
Input:  s = "rat", t = "car"
Output: false

✅Approach
Create one HashMap for s and one for t.
Count how many times each character occurs.
If their lengths are different, return false.
Compare both maps.
If both maps contain exactly the same character-frequency pairs, return true.

Code
class Solution {
    public boolean isAnagram(String s, String t) {
        HashMap<Character, Integer> mapS = new HashMap<>();
        HashMap<Character, Integer> mapT = new HashMap<>();
        if(s.length() != t.length()){
            return false;
        }
        for(int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++){
            char ch = s.charAt(i);
            char ch2 = t.charAt(i);
            mapS.put(ch, mapS.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);
            mapT.put(ch2, mapT.getOrDefault(ch2, 0) + 1);
        }
        return mapS.equals(mapT);
    }
}

Time Complexity
O(n)
We go through the strings once.
Space Complexity
O(n)
The HashMaps store the characters and their frequencies.
